  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\n\nToday (Saturday) through Tuesday...\n\nFollowing the first freeze of the season earlier this morning, \ntemperatures have made a quick recovery today back into the low to \nmid 60s. This warmup has and will continue to be assisted by steady \nsouth to southwesterly warm air advecting winds across the next \nseveral days. Though temperatures overnight tonight may not fall as \nlow as this past morning (overnight lows: mid 30s to mid 40s), a few \nareas of frost may still be able to form tonight across portions of \nthe area west or north of the Tri-Cities.\n\nAloft, ridging across the Intermountain West Region shifts east and \nover into the Central U.S., clearing skies and suppressing any \nchances for precipitation from subsidence. Winds speeding near 15-\n20MPH and gusting as high as 25-35MPH this afternoon come as \nyesterday's strong high pressure center leaves behind a compressed \nsurface pressure gradient. These occasionally gusty southerly winds \nshould maintain across the Sunday-Tuesday afternoon timeframe as \nwell, helping highs break into the upper 60s to low 70s for mainly \nSunday and Tuesday. A few clouds passing through the area Monday \ncould keep highs in the 60s (only a 20% chance of exceedance). \n\n\nWednesday and Beyond...\n\nLong-range forecast models (LREF members) continue to show \nconsiderable agreement with the general timing and intensity of how \nthis upper-level ridge progresses through the middle of next week. \nAn intensification of this ridge across the middle of the week \nshould translate down to the surface as temperatures will be likely \nto expand up into the mid 70s to lower 80s Wednesday-Friday. The \nnext pattern chance looks to come near the end of next week as a \nbuilding trough works to push the ridging pattern out. In addition, \nthis disturbance will likely bring the area its next precipitation \npotential (30-50% chance of precipitation Friday/Saturday). Given \nthe absence if any strong surface pressure gradient underneath the \nridging pattern, broad southerly winds will be favored to prevail \nacross much of the period.",
